the Turkish island of gotcha towers over  the Aegean Sea the islands Greek name is  Imbros Dimitri's Yakima's is an ethnic  Greek every morning when he unlocks the  door of the primary school he feels he's  travelling 50 years back in time this is  where he learned to read and write a few  weeks ago he became the school's janitor  a fellow pupil from his school days has  come to visit the news about the newly  opened Greek school spread fast  the men are excited wasn't this the way  to the coal cellar it's like being back  in school it's just like it was back  then until 1923 when the island became  part of Turkey only Greeks lived here  but anchors policies in the mid century  made Greek residents leave by the  thousands for decades the Greek school  in the village of iya Teodor II was  deserted now the Turkish state has let  it reopened and is paying the teachers  this taxi takes all the pupils to school  in the morning there are just four of  them the youngest is five their parents  have returned from Greece and moved back  into their family homes they left long  ago due to the Greek Turkish tensions  but the school has given them new hope  the pupils learn reading writing and  arithmetic in Turkish and Greek the  principal of the school is an ethnic  Greek who has also returned to the  island of her childhood her deputy is a  Turk as is usual in minority schools in  Turkey  four students and three teachers that's  something that needs getting used to but  I believe that it will progress as long  as we do good work and the situation for  young Greek families improves then I'm  sure that more pupils will come to our  little school  for years a Greek Orthodox foundation  fought to have the school reopened it is  seen as a symbol of the Greek  community's determination to survive and  these Greek school kids have already  gotten used to being a tiny minority I  like it I don't want more kids in my  class the village priests would like to  offer Christian religious instruction in  the school but that is not allowed his  father and grandfather were also clerics  in the village but he says past  conflicts should be laid to rest even  back when the congregation hardly  existed no one ever touched the church  not even at the worst of times when  there were fanatics on the other side I  always wanted to return some day no I'm  happy to be back in the 1960s and 70s  Turkish nationalists who blamed Greece  for the Cyprus dispute forced to the  ethnic Greeks to emigrate today the  Turkish state has adopted a more  tolerant stance towards its minorities  expropriated property is being returned  and the right to schooling and minority  languages has been strengthened Ankara  is aiming to improve its image our Prime  Minister is solving these issues like a  truly great statesman we want more  former Greek residents to return to the  island they should be able to do  business here again Greece is suffering  from a serious economic crisis and they  can make a fresh start here let us all  live together peacefully not everyone  here in the Greek village of ayat adore  is convinced that times are changing  despite the reopened school  older residents still recall the bad  times Dmitry's Yakima says he is glad he  spent the past 30 years in Greece in  Germany after the military coup in the  1980s this was a restricted area  you could only enter the island with the  passport people here were cut off from  the outside world and the government  built an open prison in the island  allowing convicts to harass the local  Greeks few people here are willing to  talk about that now  but now Greek life is reviving in Imbros  houses an ayat a Odori have been  renovated there is even a small hotel  Dimitri's yakumo's and the other  returnees are mainly pinning their hopes  in the small village school they see it  as a test of how serious Turkey is about  its new policy toward minorities
 

